  abstract={Hybrid AC/DC systems facilitate renewable energy integration, whose secure operation crucially depends on real-time and accurate state estimation. However, traditional physics-model-based methods suffer from low computational efficiency, while data-driven methods face limitations in data dependency and poor noise resistance. To address this, this paper proposes a state estimation method for hybrid AC/DC systems based on Physics-Informed Graph Sample and Aggregate (GraphSAGE). Firstly, the graph structure representation of the hybrid AC/DC system is constructed, and heterogeneous AC/DC measurement information is homogenized. Secondly, node and edge measurement features are merged, and a multi-head graph attention mechanism is introduced into the feature aggregation process of the GraphSAGE model to fully capture the coupling relationships between AC and DC nodes. Subsequently, AC/DC measurement equations and converter station power balance equations are embedded into the improved GraphSAGE model as hard constraints, guiding the model to output physically consistent estimation results. Finally, simulation experiments are conducted on a hybrid AC/DC grid incorporating a 3-terminal DC system. The results indicate that the proposed method achieves improvements in estimation accuracy, computational efficiency, and generalization capability compared to existing methods.},
